rtant thing here is the  predictors are typically going to be  denoted by  uh x is the matrix then x1 x2 x3  are the individual uh predictors and  then  y is your response vector now i really  want to emphasize one of the key  assumptions here  so take your response y and then just  pick any  predictor call that x1 so  the assumption is in the population at  large  there is a true slope parameter let's  call that  beta1 here that explains linearly  the variation in y in terms of a one  unit change in  x1 that's the assumption  now it's just like any other parameter  we can't truly know that slope parameter  but through linear regression we can  come up with an estimate for that slope  parameter  that's our estimate beta hat one  so that's a little bit different if you  have a bayesian interpretation  of statistics but the difference between  the frequentist interpretation and  bayesian interpretation  is completely the subject not even for  one more tutorial but for a whole other  series  but for how most people approach this  which is the frequent tested  interpretation  that's the true assumption on the back  end and so  you're going to get regression output  which is going to return  your estimates of these slope parameters  that is  let's just call that a vector beta hat  that's got  individual estimates beta hat 1 beta hat  2  so on and so forth naturally any  estimate or any statistic is going to  carry with it a corresponding standard  error  and then putting those things together  you can create a test statistic  and a p-value so the test statistic for  linear regression is a t-statistic  for logistic regression it's a  z-statistic and you're testing a  hypothesis of that true  corresponding slope parameter being  different  than zero so think about this if the  true slope parameter  is zero that means as x changes  y is not going to change so there's no  true relationship at least in a linear  sense  between these two variables now if that  null hypothesis 