ters  are different than zero meaning in the  population at large  there is a statistically significant  effect that these  that these variables have on our  response variable mpg  now statistically significant does not  necessarily mean the same thing as  practically significant  that's a whole uh other can of worms  there  but that's the interpretation behind  these t statistics and the corresponding  p  values now let's wrap up with these last  few terms at the bottom here  so starting with residual standard error  this is a super important quantity  now this is an estimate of the standard  deviation of the true population errors  but think of it analogously as how wrong  the model is on average so  it's also in the case of linear  regression it's equal to the quantity  root mean squared error and when your  goal is prediction  this is a really good goal post for the  quality of your model  now the lower this thing is obviously  the better  because that's telling you your model is  less wrong  on average uh the lower this thing gets  moving on we've got multiple r squared  this is a quantity known as the  coefficient of determination  and this is the proportion of the  variation in the response variable  that can be explained by this linear  regression model that we're using  so very important quantity we're  basically saying  89.3 percent of the variation  in this mpg variable is explained by  this model that we've built  but there is one very important drawback  to this  quantity which is as we add more  predictors  this thing is either going to stay the  same or it's going to keep going  up and up and up having said that adding  more predictors  is not necessarily a good thing because  it means you're just going to create  a regression model that over fits so to  correct for this problem  you've also got this quantity called  adjusted r  squared so this corrects for this issue  and the formula for it can be seen here  adjusted r squared equals  1 minus then in parenthesis 1 minus r  squared  times n minus 1 all over n minus p minus  1 where  n is the total sample size and p  is the number of predictors and  basically what's going on here is  this quantity penalizes additional  predictors  so we're balancing here explaining all  the variation  in our y variable with how much are we  overfitting how complicated are we  making this model  lastly we've got this f statistic and  now  interestingly suppose we had only one  predictor in the model  actually that f statistic would just be  equal to the square  of that corresponding t statistic but  generally speaking this isn't really  something you have to worry about that  much  you are testing whether or not the  linear regression model fits the data  better than a model with no predictors  notice that we have an extremely low p  value here  as we expect so again a high f statistic  and a corresponding low p  value would just indicate the model does  fit the data better  than a model with no predictors now this  is again  not something that you have to worry  about all that much that covers it for  linear regression and now we're going to  move on to logistic regression  so the key difference between the two is  that with  linear regression we had a continuous  response variable  now with logistic regression we have a  categorical  response variable now the thing with  logistic regression  when most people talk about it they're  specifically referring to binomial  logistic regression  that is the case where your response  variable  is a categorical variable with two  levels  typically we're going to refer to those  as success and failure  or one and zero where you've got a  probability of success  that's equal to p and then your  corresponding probability of failure  is equal to one minus p now  a term just to understand the theory  here at a high level to be familiar with  is odds now that's a ratio of the  relative probabilities  that is p divided by one minus  p so for instance suppose i'm rolling a  six sided die  the probability that i'm gonna land on a  six  is one over six but these odds here  are actually one over six divided by  five over six that is one over five  so another key assumption here  just to be familiar with is the fact  that with linear regression  your continuous variable could go all  the way from negative infinity  to infinity so that's obviously not the  case with probabilities or with odds  you're gonna go from zero to one however  what goes on with logistic regression is  you get around that issue  by taking a transformation known as the  log  odds of the probability so the log odds  is just  the natural log of p divided by 1 minus  p  and this quantity here the log odds is  sometimes known as the  the logit and so our logistic regression  equation  is just natural the logic of p  equals same thing as it did before we've  got all of our x variables  and we've got corresponding slope  estimates to them  so logistic regression is significantly  more technically and mathematically  complex than linear regression  but the high level idea behind it all  the same  most stakeholders are going to  understand  now one concept that i want to introduce  here at at least a high level  and again this may go over your head a  little bit if you don't  know statistics too much already but  just be familiar with the terminology at  a very high level  is the deviance so the deviance is  defined as  the degree to which the likelihood  that's again a statistical concept  of the saturated model exceeds the  likelihood  of the model that we're creating here  where the saturated model  is just a model with as many parameters  as you have observations  that is a perfect fit type of model  so just at a very high level just  understand  the lower the deviance that we have the  better  the higher quality of fit that we have  so  again i'm going to create the exact same  logistic regression model that i did in  our tutorial number 10  and now let's look at the output here  first thing you've got  under the model call is the deviance  residuals  and these are completely analogous to  the residuals that you saw from linear  regression  every deviance residual corresponds to  an observation  and how much it contributed to the total  deviance  that is the variation from the perfect  fit that's all you've got to understand  with that  we've got this coefficient table here  and again it's got a very  similar sort of interpretation to the  linear regression coefficient table  we've got a linear equation going on on  the back end  so let's just take for example the  income variable here  notice that there's a slope estimate  here of 0.34592  so for a one unit increase in the income  variable  we expect the log odds of the success  of a response variable here that is a  credit  card being issued we expect the log odds  of that to go up by  0.34592  now you can do a conversion there to see  how the probability changes  from unit to unit but at its highest  level  just understand that if you have a  positive coefficient here  then then as income increases we do  expect the probability of our response  variable  the credit card being issued to go up as  well  then lastly we have some more metrics  indicating the quality of the model fit  so starting with this null deviance  that's generally not something you're  going to have to worry about  that is the deviance in the situation  where you have a model  containing only the intercept so  you would expect the model with only an  intercept term  is not going to fit very well as a  result you're going to have a very high  deviance and meanwhile you've got the  residual deviance  which indicates the goodness of fit for  the model that we created  now we expect this to be much lower  if we have a good model than the null  deviance  so in this case that appears to be the  case  now then you've got aic so aic  is also a quantity that you can get from  linear regressi